-- je54-seat-powers-and-penalties: the questions a reader of an enactment asks about references (Articles 9 and 10), powers and appointments (Articles 11 and 11A), offences and penalties (Articles 12 and 13), forms
-- (Article 14), distances (Article 15) and, for the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only, the effect of repeal (Article 6), the exercise of powers before commencement (Article 16) and repeal and
-- re-enactment (Article 17).  The first seat module, je54-seat, covers the rest.
-- Every export takes the day the question is about and reads the text of the Law in force on that day.  A day before 1 January 2019 refuses for all of them (no text is deposited); a day from 28 September 2021 refuses for
-- Articles 6, 11(3), 11(4), 16 and 17 (deleted by the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021) and a day before it refuses for Article 11A (inserted by the same Law).  Where a date of the enactment is needed it is a separate input.
-- Where the Law leaves a point open the export refuses with the reason (that is the answer, not an error).

§ `Articles 9 and 10: references to enactments`


-- src:41 | (1) A reference in a Law to a Part, Article or Schedule by number only and without further identification is a reference to the Part, Article or Schedule of that number in that Law.
-- src:42 | (2) A reference in an Article or other division of a Law to a paragraph, sub-paragraph, clause or sub-clause by number or letter only and without further identification is a reference to the paragraph, sub-paragraph, clause or sub-clause of that number or letter in the Article or other division of that Law.
-- src:44 | (4) This Article shall apply, with the necessary changes, to a subordinate enactment as it applies to a Law.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 9(1), 9(2) and 9(4) (src:41-42), (src:44)
@export Find the Part, Article, Schedule, paragraph, sub-paragraph, clause or sub-clause that a reference by number or letter alone leads to, under Article 9(1), 9(2) and 9(4)
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    r IS A `Reference by number or letter` @desc The reference: the kind of division it names, the number or letter, whether it says more than that, and the instrument and division that contain it
GIVETH A `Place referred to`
`where does the reference by number or letter lead` `the day the question is about` r MEANS
    `Article 9(1) and 9(2): the place that a reference by number or letter alone leads to` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) r

-- src:43 | (3) Unless the context otherwise requires, a reference in a Law to an enactment is a reference to that enactment as amended, revised or otherwise consolidated (within the meaning of the Legislation (Jersey) Law 2021) from time to time and includes a reference to that enactment as extended or applied under another enactment, including another provision of that Law.[11]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 9(3) (src:43)
@export Say whether a reference in a Law to an enactment is a reference to the enactment in a given form: as amended, as revised or otherwise consolidated (from 28 September 2021), or as extended or applied under another enactment, unless the context otherwise requires
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    form IS A `Form in which an enactment is meant` @desc The form in which the enactment might be meant
    `the context otherwise requires` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the context of the reference requires it to be read otherwise
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the reference to an enactment a reference to it in this form` `the day the question is about` form `the context otherwise requires` MEANS
    `Article 9(3): a reference to an enactment is a reference to it in the form` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) form `the context otherwise requires`

-- src:44 | (4) This Article shall apply, with the necessary changes, to a subordinate enactment as it applies to a Law.
-- src:45 | (5) This Article shall apply to all enactments, including this Law, whenever passed or made.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 9(4) and 9(5) (src:44-45)
@export Find how Article 9 applies to an instrument, whenever it was passed or made: as it stands for a Law, with the necessary changes for a subordinate enactment
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    k IS A `Kind of instrument` @desc The kind of instrument
    `the day on which the enactment was passed or made` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the enactment was passed or made; the Article applies whenever it was, so the answer does not depend on it
GIVETH A `How Article 9 applies to an instrument`
`does Article 9 apply to the instrument` `the day the question is about` k `the day on which the enactment was passed or made` MEANS
    `Article 9(4) and 9(5): how the Article applies to an enactment passed or made on any day` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) k `the day on which the enactment was passed or made`

-- src:48 | Where any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, confers power to pass any other enactment, expressions used in that other enactment if it is passed after the commencement of this Law, shall, unless the contrary intention appears, have the same respective meanings as in the enactment conferring the power.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 10 (src:48)
@export Say whether the expressions used in an enactment have the same meanings as in the enactment conferring the power to pass it, under Article 10: they do if it was passed after the commencement of the Law on 21 August 1954, unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers power to pass the other enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment concerned confers power to pass the other enactment
    `the day on which the other enactment was passed` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the other enactment was passed or made
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 10 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`do the expressions in the other enactment have the same meaning as in the enabling enactment` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers power to pass the other enactment` `the day on which the other enactment was passed` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 10: the expressions used in the other enactment have the same meanings as in the enactment conferring the power` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers power to pass the other enactment` `the day on which the other enactment was passed` `a contrary intention appears`

§ `Articles 11 and 11A: powers, duties and appointments`

-- src:50 | (1) Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, confers a power or imposes a duty, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the power may be exercised and the duty shall be performed from time to time as occasion requires.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 11(1) (src:50)
@export Say whether a power may be exercised, or a duty must be performed, on an occasion that requires it, from time to time, under Article 11(1), unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers a power or imposes a duty` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment confers a power or imposes a duty
    `the power or duty has been used or performed before` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the power has been exercised or the duty performed before; the answer does not depend on it
    `occasion requires it again` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the occasion requires the power to be exercised or the duty performed
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 11(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the power be exercised or the duty performed on this occasion` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers a power or imposes a duty` `the power or duty has been used or performed before` `occasion requires it again` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 11(1): the power may be exercised or the duty must be performed on this occasion` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers a power or imposes a duty` `the power or duty has been used or performed before` `occasion requires it again` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:51 | (2) Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, confers a power or imposes a duty on the holder of an office as such, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the power may be exercised and the duty shall be performed by the holder for the time being of the office, or, in case of his or her absence or incapacity, by the person for the time being performing the duty of that office.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 11(2) (src:51)
@export Find who exercises a power or performs a duty conferred on the holder of an office as such, under Article 11(2): the holder for the time being, or, if the holder is absent or incapacitated, the person for the time being performing the duty of the office
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers the power or imposes the duty on the holder of an office as such` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment confers the power or imposes the duty on the holder of an office as such
    holder IS A STRING @desc The holder for the time being of the office
    `the holder is absent or incapacitated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the holder is absent or incapacitated
    `the person for the time being performing the duty of the office` IS A MAYBE STRING @desc The person for the time being performing the duty of the office, if there is one; leave empty if there is none
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 11(2) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A STRING
`who exercises the power or performs the duty of the office` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers the power or imposes the duty on the holder of an office as such` holder `the holder is absent or incapacitated` `the person for the time being performing the duty of the office` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 11(2): who exercises the power or performs the duty of the office` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers the power or imposes the duty on the holder of an office as such` holder `the holder is absent or incapacitated` `the person for the time being performing the duty of the office` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:52 | (3) Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, confers a power to pass any other enactment, the power shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be construed as including a power, exercisable in the like manner and subject to the like consent and conditions, if any, to repeal, rescind, amend or vary that other enactment.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 11(3), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:52)
@export Say whether a power to pass an enactment includes a power to repeal, rescind, amend or vary it, under Article 11(3), which is in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers a power to pass another enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment confers a power to pass another enactment
    act IS AN `Act on an enactment` @desc The act on the other enactment that is asked about
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 11(3) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`does the power to pass an enactment include the power to repeal or amend it` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers a power to pass another enactment` act `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 11(3): the power to pass the other enactment includes the power` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers a power to pass another enactment` act `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:53 | (4) Where an enactment passed after 28th March 2003 confers a power to make any other enactment, the power may, unless the contrary intention appears, be exercised –
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:54 | (a) either in relation to all cases to which the power extends, or in relation to all those cases subject to specified exceptions, or in relation to any specified cases or classes of case; and
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:55 | (b) so as to make, as respects the cases in relation to which it is exercised –
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:56 | (i) the full provision to which the power extends or any lesser provision (whether by way of exception or otherwise),
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:57 | (ii) the same provision for all cases in relation to which the power is exercised, or different provision for different cases or classes of case, or different provision as respects the same case or class of case for different purposes of the enactment,
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:58 | (iii) any such provision either unconditionally or subject to any specified condition.[9]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 11(4), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:53-58)
@export Say whether a power to make an enactment, conferred by an enactment passed after 28 March 2003, may be exercised in a given way, under Article 11(4), which is in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers a power to make another enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment confers a power to make another enactment
    `the day on which the enactment conferring the power was passed` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the enactment conferring the power was passed
    way IS A `Way a power to make an enactment is exercised` @desc The way in which the power is to be exercised
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 11(4) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the power to make an enactment be exercised in this way` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers a power to make another enactment` `the day on which the enactment conferring the power was passed` way `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 11(4): the power may be exercised in the way` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers a power to make another enactment` `the day on which the enactment conferring the power was passed` way `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:55 | (1) If an enactment confers power to appoint a person to an office, unless the contrary is expressed the power includes power –
-- src:56 | (a) to determine the terms and conditions of appointment;
-- src:57 | (b) to remove or suspend a person from the office;
-- src:58 | (c) to reappoint or reinstate a person to the office; and
-- src:59 | (d) in the circumstances set out in paragraph (2), to appoint a person to the office in place of the last holder of the office.
-- src:60 | (2) The circumstances for the purposes of paragraph (1)(d) are where –
-- src:61 | (a) the office is vacant;
-- src:62 | (b) the person holding the office has been removed or suspended from office;
-- src:63 | (c) the person holding the office is absent temporarily or permanently; or
-- src:64 | (d) the person holding the office is incapacitated temporarily or permanently in a way that affects the person’s performance.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 11A (src:55-64)
@export Say whether a power to appoint a person to an office includes a related power: to fix terms and conditions, to remove or suspend, to reappoint or reinstate, or to appoint in place of the last holder in the circumstances of Article 11A(2); in the text from 28 September 2021 only
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment confers power to appoint a person to an office` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment confers power to appoint a person to an office
    power IS A `Power related to an appointment` @desc The related power that is asked about
    c IS A `Circumstances of an office` @desc The circumstances of the office: vacant, holder removed or suspended, holder absent, holder incapacitated
    `the contrary is expressed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contrary is expressed in the enactment concerned; unlike the other Articles, Article 11A asks that it be expressed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`does the power to appoint to an office include this related power` `the day the question is about` `the enactment confers power to appoint a person to an office` power c `the contrary is expressed` MEANS
    `Article 11A: the power to appoint to an office includes the power` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment confers power to appoint a person to an office` power c `the contrary is expressed`

§ `Articles 12 and 13: offences and penalties`

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
@export Say whether an offender may be prosecuted and punished under a given enactment or the customary law when an act or omission is an offence under two or more enactments, or under an enactment and the customary law, under Article 12
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission` @desc The offences that the act or omission constitutes: the enactment that creates each offence under an enactment, and whether it is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey
    route IS A STRING @desc The enactment, by name exactly as listed, or the words the customary law of Jersey
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 12 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the offender be prosecuted and punished under this enactment` `the day the question is about` o route `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 12: the offender is liable to be prosecuted and punished under` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) o route `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:66 | Where an act or omission constitutes an offence under 2 or more enactments, or both under an enactment and under the customary law of Jersey, whether any such enactment was passed before or after the commencement of this Law, the offender shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be liable to be prosecuted and punished under either or any of those enactments or under the customary law, but shall not be liable to be punished twice for the same offence.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 12 (src:66)
@export Say whether an offender is still liable to be punished for an act or omission that is an offence under two or more enactments, or an enactment and the customary law, or has already been punished for the same offence, under Article 12
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    o IS AN `Offences arising from one act or omission` @desc The offences that the act or omission constitutes: the enactment that creates each offence under an enactment, and whether it is also an offence under the customary law of Jersey
    `the offender has already been punished for the same offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the offender has already been punished for the same offence, which is for you to judge; the Law does not say what the same offence is
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 12 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the offender liable to be punished again for the same offence` `the day the question is about` o `the offender has already been punished for the same offence` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 12: the offender is liable to be punished` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) o `the offender has already been punished for the same offence` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:68 | (1) Where, in an enactment, a penalty is specified in respect of an offence, unless the contrary intention appears, the offence shall be punishable by a penalty not exceeding the penalty specified.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(1) (src:68)
@export Say whether an offence is punishable by a proposed penalty, which it is if the proposed penalty does not exceed the penalty specified in the enactment, under Article 13(1)
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment specifies a penalty for the offence
    `the penalty specified` IS A NUMBER @desc The penalty specified, in a unit such as years of imprisonment or pounds
    `the penalty proposed` IS A NUMBER @desc The penalty proposed, in the same unit
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 13(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the offence punishable by the penalty proposed` `the day the question is about` `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` `the penalty specified` `the penalty proposed` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 13(1): the offence is punishable by the penalty proposed` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `a penalty is specified in respect of the offence` `the penalty specified` `the penalty proposed` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:69 | (2) Where a penalty for an offence is a fine and the amount of the fine or a level on the standard scale is not specified, the fine shall be construed as a fine of an unlimited amount.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(2) (src:69)
@export Say whether a fine for an offence is of an unlimited amount, which it is where neither an amount nor a level on the standard scale is specified, under Article 13(2)
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the penalty is a fine` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the penalty for the offence is a fine
    how IS A `How a fine is specified` @desc Whether an amount, a level on the standard scale, or neither is specified
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the fine of an unlimited amount` `the day the question is about` `the penalty is a fine` how MEANS
    `Article 13(2): the fine is of an unlimited amount` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the penalty is a fine` how

-- src:70 | (3) Where, in an enactment, more than one penalty is specified for an offence, the use of the word “and” shall, unless the contrary intention appears, mean that the penalties may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 13(3) (src:70)
@export Say whether several penalties joined by the word and may be imposed alternatively or cumulatively, under Article 13(3), unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment specifies more than one penalty for the offence and joins them with the word and
    mode IS A `Mode of imposing several penalties` @desc Whether the penalties are to be imposed alternatively or cumulatively
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 13(3) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the penalties be imposed alternatively or cumulatively` `the day the question is about` `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` mode `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 13(3): the penalties may be imposed` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `more than one penalty is specified and the word and is used` mode `a contrary intention appears`

§ `Articles 14 and 15: forms and distances`

-- src:73 | Where a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment, deviations from that form not materially affecting the substance nor likely to mislead shall not invalidate the form used.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 14 (src:73)
@export Find the effect of a deviation from a form prescribed or specified by an enactment under Article 14: a deviation that does not materially affect the substance and is not likely to mislead does not invalidate the form used
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an enactment prescribes or specifies the form
    `the form used deviates from it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the form used deviates from it
    `the deviation materially affects the substance` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deviation materially affects the substance, which is for you to judge
    `the deviation is likely to mislead` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the deviation is likely to mislead, which is for you to judge
GIVETH AN `Effect of a deviation from a form`
`does the deviation from the prescribed form invalidate the form used` `the day the question is about` `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` `the form used deviates from it` `the deviation materially affects the substance` `the deviation is likely to mislead` MEANS
    `Article 14: the effect of the deviation from the form` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `a form is prescribed or specified by an enactment` `the form used deviates from it` `the deviation materially affects the substance` `the deviation is likely to mislead`

-- src:75 | In the measurement of any distance for the purposes of any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, that distance shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be measured in a straight line on a horizontal plane.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 15 (src:75)
@export Say whether a distance was measured the way Article 15 requires for the purposes of an enactment, in a straight line on a horizontal plane, unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    d IS A `Distance as measured` @desc The distance, its unit, and the way it was measured
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 15 allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`was the distance measured the way the Law requires` `the day the question is about` d `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 15: the distance as measured is the distance for the purposes of the enactment` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) d `a contrary intention appears`

§ `Articles 6, 16 and 17: in the text of 1 January 2019 to 27 September 2021 only`

-- These refuse for a day from 28 September 2021, with the reason.

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:26 | (1) Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, repeals a repealing enactment, it shall not be construed as reviving any enactment previously repealed, unless words are added reviving that enactment.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 6(1), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:26)
@export Say whether the repeal of a repealing enactment is not construed as reviving an enactment previously repealed, unless words are added reviving it, under Article 6(1)
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment repeals a repealing enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment repeals a repealing enactment
    `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is the repeal of a repealing enactment barred from reviving the earlier enactment` `the day the question is about` `the enactment repeals a repealing enactment` `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed` MEANS
    `Article 6(1): the repeal is not construed as reviving the enactment previously repealed` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment repeals a repealing enactment` `words are added reviving the enactment previously repealed`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:27 | (2) Where an enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, repeals wholly or partially any former enactment and substitutes provisions for the enactment repealed, the repealed provisions shall remain in force until the substituted provisions come into operation.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 6(2), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:27)
@export Say whether provisions repealed and replaced by an enactment remain in force at an instant, until the substituted provisions come into operation, under Article 6(2)
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment substitutes provisions for those it repeals` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment repeals provisions and substitutes provisions for them
    `the instant asked about` IS A DATETIME @desc The instant asked about, in ISO 8601 form
    `the instant at which the substituted provisions come into operation` IS A MAYBE DATETIME @desc The instant at which the substituted provisions come into operation; leave empty if they have not yet
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`do the repealed provisions remain in force at the instant` `the day the question is about` `the enactment substitutes provisions for those it repeals` `the instant asked about` `the instant at which the substituted provisions come into operation` MEANS
    `Article 6(2): the repealed provisions remain in force` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment substitutes provisions for those it repeals` `the instant asked about` `the instant at which the substituted provisions come into operation`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:28 | (3) Where an enactment which has been amended by any other enactment is repealed, such repeal shall, unless the contrary intention appears, include the repeal of all those provisions of other enactments by which the first-mentioned enactment was amended.[6]
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 6(3), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:28)
@export Say whether the repeal of an enactment that was amended by other enactments includes the repeal of the provisions that amended it, under Article 6(3), unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `an enactment that has been amended by another enactment is repealed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether an enactment that has been amended by another enactment is repealed
    `the provision is one of those by which the repealed enactment was amended` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the provision asked about is one of those that amended the repealed enactment
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 6(3) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`does the repeal of an amended enactment include the repeal of its amendments` `the day the question is about` `an enactment that has been amended by another enactment is repealed` `the provision is one of those by which the repealed enactment was amended` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 6(3): the repeal includes the repeal of the provision` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `an enactment that has been amended by another enactment is repealed` `the provision is one of those by which the repealed enactment was amended` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:71 | (1) Where any Law passed after the commencement of this Law is not to come into operation immediately on the passing thereof, and confers power to make any appointment, to pass any enactment, to give notices, to prescribe forms or to do any other thing for the purposes of the Law, that power may, unless the contrary intention appears, be exercised at any time after the passing of the Law, so far as may be necessary or expedient for the purpose of bringing the Law into operation at the date of commencement, subject to the restriction that any enactment passed under the power shall not, unless the contrary intention appears in the Law, or the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into operation, come into operation until the Law comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:72 | (2) This Article shall apply in relation to Regulations passed by the States in like manner as it applies in relation to a Law.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 16(1) and 16(2), as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:71-72)
@export Say whether a power conferred by a Law (or Regulations passed by the States) that is not to come into force immediately may be exercised after the passing of the Law and before it comes into force, under Article 16
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    instrument IS AN `Instrument dealt with by Article 16` @desc Whether the instrument is a Law passed after the commencement of the Law, or Regulations passed by the States
    `the day on which it was passed` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the Law or the Regulations were passed
    `it is not to come into operation immediately on its passing and it confers a power` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether it is not to come into force immediately on its passing and confers a power to do a thing for its purposes
    `the day on which the power is exercised` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the power is exercised
    `the exercise is necessary or expedient for bringing it into operation at the date of commencement` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the exercise is necessary or expedient for bringing the Law into force on its date of commencement, which is for you to judge
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 16(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the power be exercised before the Law comes into force` `the day the question is about` instrument `the day on which it was passed` `it is not to come into operation immediately on its passing and it confers a power` `the day on which the power is exercised` `the exercise is necessary or expedient for bringing it into operation at the date of commencement` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 16(1) and 16(2): the power may be exercised before the Law comes into operation` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) instrument `the day on which it was passed` `it is not to come into operation immediately on its passing and it confers a power` `the day on which the power is exercised` `the exercise is necessary or expedient for bringing it into operation at the date of commencement` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:71 | (1) Where any Law passed after the commencement of this Law is not to come into operation immediately on the passing thereof, and confers power to make any appointment, to pass any enactment, to give notices, to prescribe forms or to do any other thing for the purposes of the Law, that power may, unless the contrary intention appears, be exercised at any time after the passing of the Law, so far as may be necessary or expedient for the purpose of bringing the Law into operation at the date of commencement, subject to the restriction that any enactment passed under the power shall not, unless the contrary intention appears in the Law, or the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into operation, come into operation until the Law comes into operation.
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:72 | (2) This Article shall apply in relation to Regulations passed by the States in like manner as it applies in relation to a Law.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 16(1) and 16(2), as they read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:71-72)
@export Say whether an enactment passed under a power exercised before the Law comes into force may itself come into force at an instant, which it may not do before the Law does unless the Law shows otherwise or the contrary is necessary, under Article 16
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    instrument IS AN `Instrument dealt with by Article 16` @desc Whether the instrument is a Law passed after the commencement of the Law, or Regulations passed by the States
    `the instant at which the enactment passed under the power is to come into operation` IS A DATETIME @desc The instant at which the enactment passed under the power is to come into force, in ISO 8601 form
    `the instant at which the Law comes into operation` IS A DATETIME @desc The instant at which the Law comes into force, in ISO 8601 form
    `a contrary intention appears in the Law` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the Law itself
    `the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into operation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into force
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`may the enactment passed under the power come into force at the instant` `the day the question is about` instrument `the instant at which the enactment passed under the power is to come into operation` `the instant at which the Law comes into operation` `a contrary intention appears in the Law` `the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into operation` MEANS
    `Article 16(1): the enactment passed under the power may come into operation at that instant` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) instrument `the instant at which the enactment passed under the power is to come into operation` `the instant at which the Law comes into operation` `a contrary intention appears in the Law` `the contrary is necessary for bringing the Law into operation`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:74 | (1) Where any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, repeals and re-enacts, with or without modification, any provisions of a former enactment, references in any other enactment to the provisions so repealed shall, unless the contrary intention appears, be construed as references to the provisions so re-enacted.
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 17(1), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:74)
@export Say whether references in other enactments to provisions repealed and re-enacted are references to the provisions re-enacted, under Article 17(1), unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment repeals and re-enacts provisions of a former enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment repeals and re-enacts, with or without modification, provisions of a former enactment
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 17(1) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is a reference to the provisions repealed a reference to the provisions re-enacted` `the day the question is about` `the enactment repeals and re-enacts provisions of a former enactment` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 17(1): a reference in another enactment to the provisions repealed is construed as a reference to the provisions re-enacted` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment repeals and re-enacts provisions of a former enactment` `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:75 | (2) Where any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, repeals any other enactment, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the repeal shall not –
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:76 | (a) revive anything not in force or not existing at the time at which the repeal takes effect;
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:77 | (b) affect the previous operation of any enactment so repealed or anything duly done or suffered under any enactment so repealed;
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:78 | (c) affect any right, privilege, obligation or liability acquired, accrued or incurred under any enactment so repealed;
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:79 | (d) affect any penalty, forfeiture or punishment incurred in respect of any offence committed against any enactment so repealed; or
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:80 | (e) affect any investigation, legal proceedings or remedy in respect of any such right, privilege, obligation, liability, penalty, forfeiture or punishment as aforesaid,
@ref Interpretation (Jersey) Law 1954, Article 17(2), as it read before 28 September 2021 (src:l_17_1954_20210927:75-80)
@export Say whether the repeal of an enactment does not do a given thing, such as reviving what was not in force or affecting rights, penalties and proceedings under the enactment repealed, under Article 17(2), unless the contrary intention appears
GIVEN
    `the day the question is about` IS A DATE @desc The day on which the fact occurred, as year-month-day; it selects the text of the Law that is read.
    `the enactment repeals another enactment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether the enactment repeals another enactment
    effect IS AN `Effect of a repeal` @desc The effect asked about
    `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Whether a contrary intention appears in the enactment concerned, which Article 17(2) allows; the Law does not say what the answer is then
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`does the repeal leave what was done, acquired, incurred or begun untouched` `the day the question is about` `the enactment repeals another enactment` effect `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    `Article 17(2): the repeal does not` (`the text of the Law in force on` `the day the question is about`) `the enactment repeals another enactment` effect `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:75 | (2) Where any enactment, whether passed before or after the commencement of this Law, repeals any other enactment, then, unless the contrary intention appears, the repeal shall not –
-- src:l_17_1954_20210927:81 | and any such investigation, legal proceeding or remedy may be instituted, continued or enforced, and any such penalty, forfeiture or punishment may be imposed as if the repealing enactment had not been passed.
